# R7.1 / N49 -- line-ending stability for the hash-pinned source bundle.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS PREVENTS
#
# `route-oracle/` and `tools/battle-oracle/` both work by slicing an executable
# prefix out of `pokelike_forked/js/bundle.deobfuscated.js` and running it in
# Node, then comparing the real JS against the Python port. Each slice's
# SHA-256 is checked in (`route-oracle/prefix.sha256`,
# `tools/battle-oracle/prefix.sha256`) and re-verified on every oracle run, so
# that a silently changed bundle cannot quietly invalidate the comparison.
#
# On Windows, `core.autocrlf=true` is a common global default. Without a
# `.gitattributes`, Git treats the bundle as text and rewrites every LF to CRLF
# on checkout. Measured on this repository: the bundle's SHA-256 goes from
#
#   dc29e7444b2883becbc336fc4cdc4dc4e823058b6cdd9b3a3dadbda400f99d82   (tracked)
#   5ac9d2e1ce56eba0b9209f70866c866d6db475116383f9fab0ad11f0c513c98d   (checkout)
#
# Both derived prefixes change with it, so both pinned hashes fail and NEITHER
# oracle can run on a fresh clone.
#
# The corruption is invisible to the usual check: `git status` reports a clean
# tree, because Git converts back to LF when it compares against the index. The
# working file on disk is what the extractor reads, and that file is wrong.
#
# THE RULE
#
# `-text` means "never do end-of-line conversion on this path", in either
# direction, regardless of the user's `core.autocrlf`. It is deliberately
# scoped to the one file whose exact bytes are pinned: the two prefixes are
# derived from it, so protecting the bundle protects them, and nothing else in
# the repository has byte-level expectations. Broadening this to `* -text`
# would also be correct for the hashes but would silently opt every future
# contributor out of line-ending normalisation for reasons unrelated to N49.
#
# Do not "fix" a hash failure here by updating `prefix.sha256`. That blesses
# the drift instead of the bytes, and the oracles stop comparing what the
# source actually is.
pokelike_forked/js/bundle.deobfuscated.js -text

# N57 -- keep the audited fixture bytes stable too. The active manifest hashes
# canonical JSON, but historical audits also carry byte-level baseline pins;
# checkout conversion must not silently move those while Git reports clean.
tools/battle-oracle/fixtures/*.json -text
tools/battle-oracle/fixtures/manifest.sha256 -text
